## Adapted fMRI quality-control paper

Copyright © 2023 Provins, MacNicol, Seeley, Hagmann and Esteban.

Provins, C., MacNicol, E., Seeley, S. H., Hagmann, P., & Esteban, O. (2023). Quality control in functional MRI studies with MRIQC and fMRIPrep. *Frontiers in Neuroimaging, 1*, 1073734. [Original publication](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnimg.2022.1073734). Licensed under [Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

`review-fmriprep-qc` adapts this work into a source-bounded QC-review workflow. Changes include ASTORIA routing, explicit researcher decisions, evidence/limitation fields and participant-specific execution checks. Preserve this credit, source and license link when redistributing that adaptation.

## MIT Communication Lab writing adaptations

- `write-introduction` adapts MIT NSE Communication Lab's [Journal Article: Introduction](https://mitcommlab.mit.edu/nse/commkit/journal-article-introduction/). Source contributors: Xinyao “Anna” Liang, Steve Jepeal and Marina Dang.
- `write-discussion` adapts Broad Research Communication Lab's [Journal Article: Discussion](https://mitcommlab.mit.edu/broad/commkit/journal-article-discussion/), MIT Communication Lab. The page names Suzanne Jacobs and states that its content was adapted from an article originally created by the MIT Biological Engineering Communication Lab.

Both sources identify their CommKit content as licensed under [Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). Preserve attribution, source and license links, identify adaptations, and retain the noncommercial condition for the sourced material. The root MIT license covers ASTORIA's original material; it does not override these source-specific terms.

ASTORIA turns the source guidance into bounded Introduction and Discussion writing skills, adding user-owned argument/status, source verification, optional CERL/MEAL review when selected, separate review roles and cross-system handoffs. The capabilities retain their individual source citations. The source authors and institutions do not endorse ASTORIA through this adaptation.

## Artwork and other sources

The main banner and isometric system icons were generated with an image-generation tool. ASTORIA supplies the editable architecture/research diagrams and system-banner compositions. Method, documentation and writing references remain cited in the relevant capabilities and templates; their inclusion as references does not transfer ownership of the external works.

The README diagrams (`agent_team`, `para_project`, `interchangeable_agents` and `system_toolkit`) are native SVG compositions with paired PNG renderings. The toolkit composition embeds eight existing generated system icons without changing their bytes; its graduation cap and ring shapes are native SVG artwork. The interchangeability illustration uses plain assistant names in original text cards; no provider logo artwork is bundled.

## Assistant names

OpenAI, Claude and Hermes Agent are used as identifying names in ASTORIA's original text cards. Names belong to their respective owners. No provider logo artwork is included, and no provider endorsement is implied.
